From 4a50a1567cdadc757de69f7c2a9263ca87d8ceae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: qiyinxi <qiyinxi@foxmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 20:50:58 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 51/65] media: tell textured frames by their textures, not
 mUseGpuTexture

The H.264 and HEVC decoders render a frame from its Y/UV textures in
getImage(), and return the textures of the frames still queued to the
pool in destroyH264Context() and destroyHevcContext(), only while
mUseGpuTexture is set. try_decode() clears it when it falls back to
FFmpeg during the trial period (createAndInitSoftVideoHelper() sets it
to false), but frames that the CUDA decoder decoded into textures
before the switch may still be queued in the MediaSnapshotHelper. Such
a frame has a texture pair and no bytes. After the switch getImage()
passes its empty data(), a null pointer, to renderToHostColorBuffer()
instead of rendering the textures, and as no swap hands a pair back,
the frame's pair leaks; on destroy the pair is skipped and leaks as
well.

Decide by the frame instead: a frame that carries a texture pair is
rendered from it and its pair is returned, whatever mUseGpuTexture
says now. Frames decoded into a byte buffer carry no texture names or
zeros and take the same path as before; check the size of the texture
vector too, since it may be empty for them.

With the guest's goldfish H.264 component the window is small: it
fetched every queued frame after each decode in our runs, so the queue
is normally empty when the next decode makes try_decode() switch.

Test: CUDA H.264 decoder in GPU texture mode, 120-frame 1080p guest
      session. A debugger cleared mUseGpuTexture before the first
      getImage(), as the fallback does, while the later frames still
      came with textures: without this change none of their pairs was
      handed back and each was "rendered" from a null pointer; with it
      118 pairs came back through the swap, as frames are rendered from
      their textures.
Test: a real fallback, forced by making good() return false in the
      middle of a session, played on with FFmpeg to the last frame. The
      queue was empty at that moment, as at every H.264 destroy we
      watched, so the destroy path was reviewed, not exercised.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:Claude-Opus-5.5
